face-image: img/inonuuu.png

= İnönü looks out the window

İsmet İnönü looked out the window. It was getting late, as evidenced by the orange and red slowly replacing the blue over the horizon. As the sun started retreating away from eyeview, the rays of light it diffused over his bedroom also began shrinking more and more. As İnönü saw, he in his decripit rocking chair would be left in darkness in a few hours, with no one to save him.

İnönü wondered to himself, "when did it come to this?". He had spent years as the right-hand man of Atatürk, balancing the ambitious and energetic man with his cautiousness and mindfulness. He remembered the good old days when the republic was declared and the joy in the eyes of everyone who believed in a better future for the Turkish people than being the subjects of a reactionary monarch. He remembered the restless nights he spent during his time as the commander in chief of the Western Front during the <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">War of Independence<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/sakarya.jpg">The Turkish War of Independence took place between 1919 and 1923 after the Entente Powers attempted to impose the harsh Treaty of Sevres onto the Turkish nation.</span></span>. Atatürk had built the nation and İnönü made sure it did not falter, as to both serve his duty for the nation he had devoted his life to and honor the legacy of his best friend.

But now he was being dumped by his own party! The party he had fostered with Kemal and led for more than 30 years! He was disappointed and angry, for he did not want things to be the way they are. Had he not done his best to not lose the spirit of his time? Had he not accepted and even pushed for the first free elections in 1950, put the party on the course of the Left of Center and accommodate Ecevit as general secretary for the sake of his energy? Hadn't he fought getting old enough? The thoughts swarmed his mind: once the defender of the progressive republic, now he was being thrown away like a relic of the past!

As he felt the rays of sunlight leave the vertex of his head, he turned his chair around and stared down the passing sun. It was beautiful. Not as straightforward as the daytime perhaps but blooming with colors of different shades, something not possible under sunlight. Perhaps that was what was needed for the republic. Even though his time may be over, perhaps the ideals he and Kemal espoused may live in another form; after all, what if his dusk was the dawn of someone else?

- @root: And he wanted to see his friend, after a long time...
